  => STEP 0 done: with probing disabled the DUT emitted no ARP and no ICMP whatever and
     offered .100 immediately, although the host stood ready to claim all 11 pool
     addresses and was never asked. This is the zero-probe control that every later step
     is read against, and it satisfies S1566.1.05. The ACK carried option 51 = 86400 s for
     `lease 1 0 0`; options 58 (renewal) and 59 (rebinding) were absent.

STEP 1 -- ARP probe burst: count and spacing must match the configuration             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TFS S1566.1.03 verbatim: "When configured for ARP probing, the DHCP server will probe as
  follows: 1. Send an ARP Request for the next available IP address. 2. Wait T msecs for a
  response, where T is the configured timeout."
  TFS S1566.1.04: "these steps are repeated N times, where N is the configured number of
  probe packets."   Configured: N = 3, T = 1000 ms.

awplus(config)# ip dhcp pool CKPOOL
awplus(dhcp-config)# probe enable
awplus(dhcp-config)# probe type arp
tb277$ sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:00:02 \
         --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --wait 20 --pcap leg2.pcap --json leg2.json
  (no --arp-answer: nothing on the segment claims the address)
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:02 xid 0x75485364
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 1 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 3 gaps=[0.995, 1.001]           <-- exactly N=3, spaced at T=1000 ms
     t=0.4434   target=192.168.10.101   from=192.168.10.1
     t=1.4388   target=192.168.10.101   from=192.168.10.1
     t=2.4399   target=192.168.10.101   from=192.168.10.1
  OFFER: 192.168.10.101
     lease_time=86400
  ACK  : ACK 192.168.10.101
     lease_time=86400 renewal=None rebinding=None

  => STEP 1 PASS: exactly three probes, not two and not five, against `probe packets 3`;
     inter-probe gaps of 0.995 s and 1.001 s against `probe timeout 1000`; all three
     sourced from the VLAN 10 SVI 192.168.10.1; and the address probed (.101) is the
     address offered. Against the zero probes of STEP 0 on identical cabling, this is the
     configured burst appearing on the wire. S1566.1.03 and the count/timing limbs of
     S1566.1.04 hold.

STEP 2 -- an answered ARP probe must prevent that address being offered               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TFS S1566.1.04 verbatim: "The probing process is exited if a response is received. The
  server will not then offer this address, and it will repeat this whole process for the
  next available address."

  Leg a -- the host claims 192.168.10.102, the next free address:
tb277$ sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:00:03 \
         --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --arp-answer 192.168.10.102 --wait 25 \
         --pcap leg3.pcap --json leg3.json
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:03 xid 0xefba3d8f
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 0 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 1                               <-- probing EXITED on the first reply
     t=0.449    target=192.168.10.102   from=192.168.10.1  <- ANSWERED
  OFFER: NONE
  ACK  : NONE                                      <-- the address was NOT offered

  Leg b -- the same chaddr retries, the host still claiming .102:
tb277$ sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:00:03 \
         --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --arp-answer 192.168.10.102 --wait 25 \
         --pcap leg3b.pcap --json leg3b.json
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:03 xid 0x89b9dc7f
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 1 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 3 gaps=[0.994, 1.001]           <-- .102 skipped, .103 probed instead
     t=0.4477   target=192.168.10.103   from=192.168.10.1
     t=1.4419   target=192.168.10.103   from=192.168.10.1
     t=2.4429   target=192.168.10.103   from=192.168.10.1
  OFFER: 192.168.10.103
     lease_time=86400
  ACK  : ACK 192.168.10.103
     lease_time=86400 renewal=None rebinding=None

  The DUT's own view of the same events:
awplus# show ip dhcp binding
  Pool CKPOOL Network 192.168.10.0/24
  DHCP Client Entries
  IP Address      ClientId                            Type    Expiry
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  192.168.10.100  02c4.0000.0001                      Dynamic 16 Oct 2026 02:23:09
  192.168.10.101  02c4.0000.0002                      Dynamic 16 Oct 2026 02:23:40
  192.168.10.102                                      Dynamic 16 Oct 2026 02:23:56
  192.168.10.103  02c4.0000.0003                      Dynamic 16 Oct 2026 02:24:47
                  ^^^ .102 is held with an EMPTY ClientId: the conflict was detected and
                      recorded, and the address belongs to no client

  => STEP 2 PASS: an address proven in use is never offered. Probing exited on the first
     reply (1 probe, not 3), no OFFER and no ACK followed, the DUT recorded .102 as a
     conflict with an empty ClientId, and the retry skipped .102 and allocated .103. Legs
     a and b differ only in which address the host claimed, so the withholding is caused by
     the answered probe and nothing else.
     Measured divergence from the spec wording: S1566.1.04 says the server "will repeat
     this whole process for the next available address", which reads as a roll-on inside
     the same transaction. It does not happen here -- the DUT sends nothing further for
     that DISCOVER and the client must re-send, which then succeeds (leg b). Harmless with
     any RFC 2131 client, since such a client always retries, but it is not what the
     sentence says.

STEP 3 -- probe type arp and probe type ping are genuinely different code paths       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TFS S1566.1.17: "ARP probe and ping probe are mutually exclusive, and this will be
  enforced by the CLI."
  TFS S1566.1.01 (ping probing, locally connected clients) verbatim: "1. A single ARP
  Request will be sent for the next available IP address. If a ARP response is received an
  ICMP Echo Request for this address will immediately be sent. 2. Wait T msecs for a
  response to the ARP Request or ICMP Echo Request."
  So under ping probing the server still ARPs first, and ICMP only follows an ARP RESPONSE:
  "probe type ping produces no ARP" is NOT the discriminator. The discriminator is an
  ANSWERED ARP, which legs b and c isolate.

  Leg a -- probe type ping, nothing answers (the baseline for legs b and c):
awplus(config)# ip dhcp pool CKPOOL
awplus(dhcp-config)# probe type ping
awplus# show ip dhcp pool
      Probe:                       Default Values
        Status:       Enabled      [Enabled]
        Type:         Ping         [Ping]          <-- the CLI selection took effect
        Packets:      3            [5]
        Timeout:      1000 msecs   [200]
tb277$ sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:00:04 \
         --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --wait 20 --pcap leg4.pcap --json leg4.json
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:04 xid 0x301676f9
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 1 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 3 gaps=[0.996, 1.001]
     t=0.4481   target=192.168.10.104   from=192.168.10.1
     t=1.4439   target=192.168.10.104   from=192.168.10.1
     t=2.4449   target=192.168.10.104   from=192.168.10.1
  OFFER: 192.168.10.104
     lease_time=86400
  ACK  : ACK 192.168.10.104
     lease_time=86400 renewal=None rebinding=None
  ICMP Echo Requests = 0, counted by dhcp4.py and by tcpdump into leg4.pcap, which agree.
  Correct per S1566.1.01: ARP first, no ARP response, so no escalation to ICMP.

  Leg b -- probe type ping, the ARP is ANSWERED but the ICMP is NOT (target .105):
  tcpdump leg5.pcap, verbatim:
    15:26:39.244415 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.105 tell 192.168.10.1
    15:26:39.283285 ARP, Reply 192.168.10.105 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
    15:26:39.283755 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.105 tell 192.168.10.1
    15:26:39.350797 ARP, Reply 192.168.10.105 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
    15:26:39.351108 IP 192.168.10.1 > 192.168.10.105: ICMP echo request, id 32995, seq 0
    15:26:40.246265 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.105 tell 192.168.10.1
    15:26:41.246618 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.105 tell 192.168.10.1
  ICMP Echo Requests = 1, against 0 in leg a  <-- the escalation of S1566.1.01 fired, and
                                                  it was triggered by the ARP response
  OFFER: 192.168.10.105 -- the address WAS offered, because no echo reply came back.

  Leg c -- probe type ping, ARP and ICMP BOTH answered (target .106):
  tcpdump leg6.pcap, verbatim:
    15:27:54.072338 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.106 tell 192.168.10.1
    15:27:54.111360 ARP, Reply 192.168.10.106 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
    15:27:54.111839 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.10.106 tell 192.168.10.1
    15:27:54.159071 ARP, Reply 192.168.10.106 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
    15:27:54.159395 IP 192.168.10.1 > 192.168.10.106: ICMP echo request, id 53476, seq 0
    15:27:54.208867 IP 192.168.10.106 > 192.168.10.1: ICMP echo reply,   id 53476, seq 0
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:06 xid 0xc90ec72
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 0 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 2 gaps=[0.049]
     t=0.4472   target=192.168.10.106   from=192.168.10.1  <- ANSWERED
     t=0.4963   target=192.168.10.106   from=192.168.10.1  <- ANSWERED
  ICMP probes seen: 1 (answered 1)
     t=0.5478   target=192.168.10.106   from=192.168.10.1  <- ANSWERED
  OFFER: NONE
  ACK  : NONE                                      <-- the address was withheld

  => STEP 3 PASS: legs b and c are a matched pair -- identical configuration, identical
     crafted DISCOVER, and the only difference is whether the ICMP echo was answered: .105
     was offered, .106 was withheld, and probing exited on the reply as S1566.1.04
     requires. Pairing STEP 2 (type arp, ARP answered => withheld) against leg b (type
     ping, ARP answered but ICMP unanswered => offered) shows the two probe types are
     different code paths and that the CLI selection takes effect, so S1566.1.01 and
     S1566.1.17 hold.
     Measured divergence from the spec wording: S1566.1.01 step 2 says the server waits T
     ms "for a response to the ARP Request or ICMP Echo Request", and S1566.1.04 says the
     address is not offered if a response is received. Under probe type ping an ARP
     response alone did not stop the offer; only the ICMP echo reply did. The behaviour is
     coherent -- under ping probing the ping is the authority -- but it is not the literal
     "or" of S1566.1.01.

STEP 4 -- the configured probe COUNT is honoured at its boundary                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)# ip dhcp pool CKPOOL
awplus(dhcp-config)# probe type arp
awplus(dhcp-config)# probe packets 0
tb277$ sudo -n python3.8 dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:c4:00:00:00:07 \
         --arp-watch 192.168.10.0/24 --arp-answer 192.168.10.107 --wait 15 \
         --pcap leg7.pcap --json leg7.json
  --- summary ---
  chaddr 02:c4:00:00:00:07 xid 0xf2f8cdb6
  sent: 1 DISCOVER, 1 REQUEST
  ARP probes seen: 0
  ICMP probes seen: 0 (answered 0)
  OFFER: 192.168.10.107
     lease_time=86400
  ACK  : ACK 192.168.10.107
     lease_time=86400 renewal=None rebinding=None

  => STEP 4 PASS: with `probe packets 0` the DUT probed not at all and handed out .107 even
     though the host was standing by to claim it -- exactly the address that would have
     been withheld in STEP 2. This is a second, independent zero-probe control, produced by
     the count rather than by disabling the feature, so the count setting is read on every
     allocation and is honoured down to its 0 boundary.

